course: chinese-street
lesson: 22
title: Days of the week, the clock, birthdays
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Chinese week is a number game: xīngqī means week, and Monday is "week-one", xīngqīyī, Tuesday week-two, all the way to Saturday, xīngqīliù, week-six.
explain: Only Sunday breaks the pattern: it is xīngqītiān or xīngqīrì, where tiān and rì both just mean "day", not week-seven.
explain: Clock time uses diǎn, "dot": "jǐ diǎn le?" is "what time is it?", "sān diǎn bàn" is half past three; but when Lín's mum says "jǐ diǎn le?" at the door with her arms crossed, it means "you're late!".
explain: Dates go big to small: month, then day, so 8 March is "sān yuè bā hào"; and some grandparents also count birthdays by the moon calendar, so their birthday moves every year.
explain: Tones matter here too: fēn (high) is a minute, but fèn (falling) is a portion — "shí fēnzhōng" is ten minutes, the most bargained-over time in any home.
fact: "xīngqīyī" means Monday, literally "week-one"; the days are numbered from Monday (1) to Saturday (6).
fact: "xīngqīliù" means Saturday, "week-six"; "zhōuliù" is another way of saying it, and many children go to Chinese school on Saturday.
fact: "xīngqītiān" means Sunday, the only day without a number; "xīngqīrì" means the same.
fact: "zhōumò" means the weekend; "zhōumò kuàilè" (happy weekend) is a common Friday goodbye.
fact: "jǐ diǎn le?" means "what time is it?" and the answer is like "sān diǎn le", it's three o'clock; diǎn means dot, o'clock.
fact: "sān diǎn bàn" means half past three; bàn means half, so "yí ge bàn xiǎoshí" is an hour and a half.
fact: "shí fēnzhōng" means ten minutes; fēn (minute) is high and level, while fèn (falling) means a portion.
fact: "chídào le" means late for school or a lesson; "kuài diǎnr, yào chídào le!" is "hurry, we'll be late!", while wǎn is just late in the day.
fact: "shénme shíhou?" means "when?"; "nǐ shénme shíhou lái?" is "when are you coming?".
fact: "shēngrì" means birthday; "nǐ de shēngrì shì jǐ yuè jǐ hào?" asks the date, and Chinese dates go month then day.
fact: Some grandparents also count birthdays by the lunar calendar, which falls on a different Western date each year.
fact: "fàngjià" means school holidays; "fàngjià le!" means "holidays have started!", shǔjià is the summer holiday and hánjià the winter holiday.
exercise: What is xīngqīyī? | Monday | Sunday morning | Saturday | Tuesday
exercise: Lín says he has Chinese school on xīngqīliù. Which day? | Saturday | Sunday | Friday | Monday
exercise: Which day has no number in its name? | Sunday, xīngqītiān | Saturday, xīngqīliù | Monday, xīngqīyī | Wednesday, xīngqīsān
exercise: Lín asks "zhōumò nǐ zuò shénme?". What does he want to know? | What you're doing at the weekend | What you're doing after school | What you did yesterday | What time you get up
exercise: On Friday Lín's teacher waves: "zhōumò kuàilè!". What is she wishing? | A happy weekend | A happy birthday | A happy new year | A happy summer holiday
exercise: You ask "jǐ diǎn le?". What did you ask? | What time is it? | What day is it today? | How old are you? | How far away is it?
exercise: Lín looks at the clock: "sān diǎn le". What time is it? | Three o'clock | Half past three | Thirteen o'clock | Three minutes past
exercise: Home time is "sān diǎn bàn". When is that? | Half past three | Three o'clock sharp | Quarter to three | Half past two, after lunch
exercise: What is "yí ge bàn xiǎoshí"? | An hour and a half | Half an hour | One and a half minutes | Two hours
exercise: Lín begs "zài wánr shí fēnzhōng!". How long? | Ten more minutes | Ten more whole hours | Ten more games of tag | Ten more seconds only
exercise: Lín's mum shouts "kuài diǎnr, yào chídào le!". What's the problem? | You'll be late for school | It's starting to rain outside | Dinner is ready and getting cold | The shop is closed
exercise: Lín asks "nǐ shénme shíhou lái?". What does he want to know? | When you're coming | Why you're coming | Who's coming | How you're coming
exercise: Lín asks "nǐ de shēngrì shì jǐ yuè jǐ hào?". What does he want to know? | The date of your birthday | How old you are this year | What present you want from him | Where your party is
exercise: Your birthday is 8 March. How do you say it? | sān yuè bā hào | bā yuè sān hào | sān hào bā yuè | bā hào sān yuè
exercise: Last day of term, Lín jumps up: "fàngjià le!". What's happened? | The holidays have started | He's late for school again | It's his birthday today | It's the weekend
exercise: What is shǔjià? | The summer holiday | The winter holiday | The weekend | A birthday party
test: my mate reckons Sunday is xīngqīqī, week-seven, cos it's the 7th day. right? | No, it's xīngqītiān or xīngqīrì | Yes, xīngqīqī is Sunday, easy | Yes, but only in China, not here | No, Sunday is xīngqīyī
test: Lín's mum stands at the door at 9 pm, arms crossed: "jǐ diǎn le?". Does she want the time? | No, she means "you're late!" | Yes, her watch has stopped working | Yes, she's lost track of time | No, she means "goodnight, love"
test: Lín's granny's birthday was 3 March last year and 22 March this year. Is someone confused? | No, she counts by the lunar calendar | Yes, Granny forgot her own birthday | Yes, she was born twice | No, she just changes it for fun
test: Lín says "shí fènzhōng" with a falling fèn. What did he mix up? | fèn means a portion; minute is fēn, high | Nothing, both tones are fine for minutes | fèn means an hour | fèn means a second, not a minute
test: Which is "late for school" and which is "late in the day"? | chídào is late for school; wǎn is late in the day | wǎn is late for school; chídào is late in the day | Both mean late for school | Both mean late in the day
test: Wednesday is week-three. Using the pattern, what is Thursday? | xīngqīsì | xīngqīsān | xīngqīwǔ | xīngqīliù
test: A British card says 4/5. Lín's dad reads it the Chinese way, month first. Which date is "sì yuè wǔ hào"? | 5 April | 4 May | 5 May | 4 April
test: Lín's mum: "fàngjià le, but hánjià is short". Which holiday is she talking about? | The winter holiday | The summer holiday | The weekend | A birthday
test: What would happen if you told Lín "xīngqīliù jiàn" on a Thursday? | He'd expect to see you on Saturday | He'd expect you tomorrow, Friday | He'd expect you on Sunday | He'd expect you on Monday
test: ugh bro it's three thirty, where are u, ur late. In Mandarin, which time is that? | sān diǎn bàn | sān diǎn le | bàn diǎn sān | sān shí diǎn
test: Which two words make "when?"? | shénme shíhou | jǐ diǎn | shí fēnzhōng | zhōumò
exam: Identify the Mandarin designation for the day of the week which, uniquely, bears no ordinal numeral. | xīngqītiān | xīngqīliù | xīngqīyī | zhōumò
exam: It is given that a pupil's birthday falls upon the eighth day of March. Select the rendering consistent with the established ordering of Chinese dates. | sān yuè bā hào | bā yuè sān hào | bā hào sān yuè | sān hào bā yuè
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phrase "jǐ diǎn le?" when uttered by a parent awaiting a tardy child at the threshold? | It functions as a reproach for lateness, not a genuine enquiry | It is a sincere request for the hour, the parent's watch having stopped | It is an invitation to dinner | It is a valediction for the night
exam: Ascertain the reason for which certain grandparents' birthdays fall on differing Western dates from year to year. | They are reckoned by the lunar calendar | They are chosen afresh annually by lot | They are moved to the nearest weekend by law | The grandparents misremember them
